    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "3fd2d808_e8eaa71e",
        "filename": "test/tablet_broken_sensor.c",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 77,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000107
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-03-15T22:59:09Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So I looked closer and I\u0027m pretty certain you want these moved to `before_test()`. Specifically, I think `before_test()` should be:\n\n```\n\t/* Make sure the device lid is in a consistent state (close). */\n\tgpio_set_level(GPIO_LID_OPEN, 0);\n\tgpio_set_level(GPIO_TABLET_MODE_L, 1);\n\t\n\tmsleep(50);\n\t\n\ttablet_hook_count \u003d 1;\n```\n\nWhy do I say this? Well, let\u0027s imagine that `test_start_lid_close()` fails midway through for some reason. I _think_ that means that `GPIO_LID_OPEN` and `GPIO_TABLET_MODE_L` could be left in any arbitrary state since `TEST_ASSERT` can cause an early return if the test fails.\n\nAfter `test_start_lid_close()` returns early then I think we\u0027ll run `test_start_tablet_mode()`, right? That\u0027s only guaranteed to run correctly if those first two commands cause a transition to tablet mode. ...but if `test_start_lid_close()` returned early then maybe the device was already in tablet mode?\n\nSaid another way, without this being in `before_test()` I think that a failure in one test could cause a cascading failure in the second test. That doesn\u0027t seem ideal. If you instead reset to the default state then you won\u0027t get a cascading failure.\n\nIf I\u0027m misunderstanding how the EC test frame works then please ignore me.",
      "parentUuid": "5faec06a_857a04c8",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 67,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 77,
        "endChar": 1
      },
      "revId": "493d627698a814ca06f7dcbaa767c0458023720c",
